--TEST--
Core\Totp::code answers six digits, Core\Totp::check answers the step it belonged to, and the step it answered refuses that same code the second time — with no argument anywhere that widens the window — ADR 0060 §§ 1, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement. `Core\Totp` has two members and the property worth pinning is
// what the pair does across a whole set of wrong answers: everything that is
// not this secret's current code is the same `null`, and the one thing that is
// becomes `null` too as soon as its step has been stored.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/totp.rs` owns why "no replay" is a counter the caller
// holds, and RFC 6238's own test vectors are that module's `#[test]`.
secret bytes $secret = Core\Random::bytes(20);
secret bytes $other = Core\Random::bytes(20);

// A code is six characters of decimal text, never a number: `042311` is a
// code, and an implementation that answered an int would show five digits
// once in ten enrolments.
string $code = Core\Totp::code($secret);
echo "digits ", Core\Str::length($code), "\n";
if (Core\Regex::matches($code, "^[0-9]{6}$")) {
    echo "decimal throughout", "\n";
} else {
    echo "not a code: ", $code, "\n";
}

// The code this second verifies, and what comes back is the step it belonged
// to rather than a `bool` — which is the whole of how the caller refuses it
// next time. The step's *value* is the clock's and is not printed; that it
// exists at all is the assertion.
// The answer's *type* is half the design: `?int` rather than `bool` is what
// makes the replay refusal expressible at all, and narrowing it is what a
// caller does before storing it.
?int $step = Core\Totp::check($code, $secret);
if ($step == null) {
    echo "its own code was refused", "\n";
} else {
    echo "accepted, and it named a step", "\n";

    // The replay, which is the entry's headline. Storing that step and passing
    // it back refuses the same code — and it is the *code* that is refused,
    // not the account: a member that remembered nothing would accept this
    // line.
    if (Core\Totp::check($code, $secret, $step) == null) {
        echo "and the second time it is refused", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "a replayed code was accepted", "\n";
    }
}

// Every other way of not being this secret's current code agrees on `null`.
// A member that threw for the malformed ones would put a mistyped code — the
// ordinary case — inside a `catch`, and one that answered a distinguishable
// value for the wrong secret would say which half of a guess landed.
array<string> $wrong = ["000000", "12345", "1234567", "", "abcdef", "01234a"];
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($wrong as string $guess) {
    if (Core\Totp::check($guess, $secret) == null) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
if (Core\Totp::check($code, $other) == null) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
echo "one refusal: ", $refused, " of ", Core\Arr::count($wrong) + 1, "\n";

// A secret below RFC 4226 § 4's 128-bit floor is a `LogicError` rather than a
// weaker deployment, on both members — the one thing about a TOTP setup that
// working with it every day would never reveal.
try {
    echo Core\Totp::code(Core\Random::bytes(8)), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "short secret refused", "\n";
}
try {
    echo Core\Totp::check($code, Core\Random::bytes(8)), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "on both members", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
digits 6
decimal throughout
accepted, and it named a step
and the second time it is refused
one refusal: 7 of 7
short secret refused
on both members
